WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.365
Lesson 6 is the capstone of M3, and it deserves a richer treatment because it asks students to coordinate everything the module has taught.

2
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:12.729
A long energy problem can involve a lift stage, a useful-yield stage, a leak, a transfer into Motion Store, and a final threshold or target.

3
00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:19.094
Students often fail these questions not because they do not know the formulas, but because they choose the formulas in the wrong order.

4
00:00:19.094 --> 00:00:25.458
That is why this lesson is really about planning.

5
00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:31.823
What quantity does the first stage create?

6
00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:38.188
What becomes the input to the next stage?

7
00:00:38.188 --> 00:00:44.552
Should the problem be solved forward from the start or backward from the target?

8
00:00:44.552 --> 00:00:50.917
The worked example becomes dramatically easier once the learner stops searching for one favorite equation and starts building a mission map.

9
00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:57.282
Keep this takeaway in view: The capstone skill in M3 is not just calculation; it is stage planning and justified equation order.

10
00:00:57.282 --> 00:01:03.646
Structure means stages.

11
00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:10.011
A long problem is made of linked mission steps, not one undivided block.

12
00:01:10.011 --> 00:01:16.375
Bridge idea means output becomes input.

13
00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:22.740
Useful output from one step often feeds the next step directly.

14
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.187
This diagram matters because it turns a long problem into a sequence of smaller decisions.

15
00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:33.635
One stage creates useful Height Store.

16
00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:39.082
Another stage loses part of it.

17
00:01:39.082 --> 00:01:44.529
Another stage turns the remaining energy into Motion Store.

18
00:01:44.529 --> 00:01:49.976
Then the final value is checked against a target or gate threshold.

19
00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:55.424
The board reminds the learner that a mixed problem is not mixed randomly.

20
00:01:55.424 --> 00:02:00.871
It has structure.

21
00:02:00.871 --> 00:02:06.318
And once that structure is visible, the equation order becomes much easier to justify.

22
00:02:06.318 --> 00:02:11.765
Keep this takeaway in view: A mission map turns long energy questions into linked steps with a defendable solving order.

23
00:02:11.765 --> 00:02:17.213
Stage map: Break the mission into meaningful steps instead of treating it as one blur of numbers.

24
00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:22.660
Bridge quantities: Intermediate results are important because they become the starting point for the next stage.

25
00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.789
Imagine planning a delivery route through several checkpoints.

26
00:02:27.789 --> 00:02:32.918
Fuel is used, some cargo may be lost, and the final destination has a minimum delivery requirement.

27
00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:38.047
No one solves that task by staring at the map and guessing one favorite number.

28
00:02:38.047 --> 00:02:43.177
They plan the route stage by stage.

29
00:02:43.177 --> 00:02:48.306
M3 Lesson 6 works the same way.

30
00:02:48.306 --> 00:02:53.435
The useful output of one stage becomes the incoming resource for the next, and the final threshold decides success or failure.

31
00:02:53.435 --> 00:02:58.564
This analogy is helpful because it makes equation order feel practical rather than arbitrary.

32
00:02:58.564 --> 00:03:03.693
Keep this takeaway in view: The route analogy helps students see equation order as a consequence of the mission structure.

33
00:03:03.693 --> 00:03:08.822
Checkpoint planning means stages matter.

34
00:03:08.822 --> 00:03:13.952
Each stage creates the conditions for the next stage.

35
00:03:13.952 --> 00:03:19.081
Losses on route means leaks matter.

36
00:03:19.081 --> 00:03:24.210
Intermediate losses change what remains available later.

37
00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:29.973
The capstone lesson leans on two general statements.

38
00:03:29.973 --> 00:03:35.736
First, total energy in equals total useful energy out plus total wasted energy.

39
00:03:35.736 --> 00:03:41.499
That is the overall mission ledger.

40
00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:47.261
Second, the output of one stage becomes the input of the next stage.

41
00:03:47.261 --> 00:03:53.024
That is the sequencing rule.

42
00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:58.787
These are powerful because they tell the student how to organize the whole problem before choosing any one equation such as mgh, kinetic energy, power, or efficiency.

43
00:03:58.787 --> 00:04:04.550
The real challenge is not remembering the formulas.

44
00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:10.313
It is knowing when each one belongs in the chain.

45
00:04:10.313 --> 00:04:16.076
Keep this takeaway in view: The capstone formulas are really planning statements that organize the whole problem before the arithmetic begins.

46
00:04:16.076 --> 00:04:21.839
Overall view: The full mission still obeys one total energy ledger even when it contains several sub-stages.

47
00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:27.601
Local view: Each stage has its own useful output, and that output can become the next stage's input.

48
00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:33.364
total energy in = total useful energy out + total wasted energy.

49
00:04:33.364 --> 00:04:39.127
output of one stage = input of the next stage.

50
00:04:39.127 --> 00:04:44.890
Treat the full problem as a chain of stages, each with an input, an output, and possibly a leak.

51
00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:49.945
A strong capstone routine is to work in four passes.

52
00:04:49.945 --> 00:04:55.001
First, map the mission stages and label the stores, transfers, leaks, and target.

53
00:04:55.001 --> 00:05:00.056
Second, chain the stages by marking what output becomes the next input.

54
00:05:00.056 --> 00:05:05.112
Third, test each stage with the appropriate equation in sequence.

55
00:05:05.112 --> 00:05:10.167
Fourth, judge the final mission by comparing the last useful amount with the target.

56
00:05:10.167 --> 00:05:15.223
This pattern matters because it transforms long questions from intimidating walls of information into a series of linked decisions.

57
00:05:15.223 --> 00:05:20.278
Keep this takeaway in view: Long energy questions become much more manageable when the learner uses a consistent mission-planning routine.

58
00:05:20.278 --> 00:05:25.334
Pass 1 means map.

59
00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:30.389
Turn the long problem into a stage-by-stage mission picture.

60
00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:35.445
Pass 2 means chain.

61
00:05:35.445 --> 00:05:40.500
Mark the bridge quantities that feed one stage into the next.

62
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.101
Try four checks.

63
00:05:44.101 --> 00:05:47.701
Is a long energy problem one giant substitution?

64
00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:51.302
No.

65
00:05:51.302 --> 00:05:54.902
Can useful output from one stage become input to the next?

66
00:05:54.902 --> 00:05:58.503
Yes.

67
00:05:58.503 --> 00:06:02.104
If a final threshold is known together with the last-stage efficiency, can working backward be sensible?

68
00:06:02.104 --> 00:06:05.704
Yes.

69
00:06:05.704 --> 00:06:09.305
And why does equation order matter?

70
00:06:09.305 --> 00:06:12.906
Because one stage often creates the quantity needed by the next stage.

71
00:06:12.906 --> 00:06:16.506
Those are the central insights of the capstone lesson.

72
00:06:16.506 --> 00:06:20.107
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the learner can now treat the whole problem as a planned mission instead of a desperate formula search.

73
00:06:20.107 --> 00:06:23.708
One giant formula?

74
00:06:23.708 --> 00:06:27.308
means no.

75
00:06:27.308 --> 00:06:30.909
The mission has stages, and each stage needs its own role in the plan.

76
00:06:30.909 --> 00:06:34.509
Bridge quantity means yes.

77
00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:38.110
Useful output can become the next stage's input directly.

78
00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:43.906
You are ready for the final worked example now.

79
00:06:43.906 --> 00:06:49.702
Start by mapping the stages of the mission and identifying the target.

80
00:06:49.702 --> 00:06:55.498
Decide whether the cleanest route is forward from the given input or backward from the final requirement.

81
00:06:55.498 --> 00:07:01.293
Mark the bridge quantity created by each stage.

82
00:07:01.293 --> 00:07:07.089
Then use the appropriate equation for each step in sequence and compare the final useful energy with the target.

83
00:07:07.089 --> 00:07:12.885
The best M3 capstone answers feel planned, not lucky.

84
00:07:12.885 --> 00:07:18.681
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the whole M3 module now comes together as one mission-planning method.

85
00:07:18.681 --> 00:07:24.477
Step 1: Turn the word problem into a stage map with stores, leaks, and target.

86
00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:30.273
Step 2: Decide which stage result becomes the bridge quantity for the next step.

87
00:07:30.273 --> 00:07:36.068
map the stages and mark the target before calculating.

88
00:07:36.068 --> 00:07:41.864
choose whether to work forward or backward through the chain.

89
00:07:41.864 --> 00:07:47.660
The strongest capstone answers make the equation order feel inevitable because the mission map made it obvious.
